WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.580 --> 00:00:05.370
From a character inspired by action star Steven
Seagal to gourmet dishes hand-crafted by an

00:00:05.370 --> 00:00:11.150
award-winning chef, the untold truth of The
Menu is chock full of tasty tidbits.

00:00:11.150 --> 00:00:15.339
The Menu is a very tightly wound film with
a lot of precision, but that doesn't mean

00:00:15.339 --> 00:00:18.100
there wasn't room for improvisation on set.

00:00:18.100 --> 00:00:22.439
Stars Anya Taylor-Joy and Nicholas Hoult revealed
that they had plenty of time to improvise

00:00:22.439 --> 00:00:23.770
together while filming.

00:00:23.770 --> 00:00:28.130
In a video segment for Vanity Fair, Taylor-Joy
explained that director Mark Mylod wanted

00:00:28.130 --> 00:00:31.950
all the actors to be improvising while the
camera was on other characters and they were

00:00:31.950 --> 00:00:33.820
sitting in the background.

00:00:33.820 --> 00:00:34.820
Taylor-Joy revealed:

00:00:34.820 --> 00:00:38.829
"We committed to the improv, like, pretty
hardcore the first couple of days."

00:00:38.829 --> 00:00:43.510
Taylor-Joy also confessed that she improvised
a line during a scene with Ralph Fiennes - and

00:00:43.510 --> 00:00:45.340
was terrified afterwards.

00:00:45.340 --> 00:00:49.580
During the scene in question, Taylor Joy's
character Margot primly tells Fiennes' character

00:00:49.580 --> 00:00:54.219
Chef Slowik "thank you" as he's leaving their
table, and it's clearly a passive-aggressive

00:00:54.219 --> 00:00:55.770
move on her part.

00:00:55.770 --> 00:00:57.280
Taylor-Joy recalls adding the line and thinking,

00:00:57.280 --> 00:01:01.960
"Oh god, Ralph Fiennes is going to think I'm
an absolute brat because that was not scripted."

00:01:01.960 --> 00:01:06.254
Mylod kept her ad-lib in the film, and she
felt vindicated as a result.

00:01:06.254 --> 00:01:11.760
John Leguizamo plays Georgie Diaz, a washed-up
action star who's pompous and arrogant.

00:01:11.760 --> 00:01:15.650
He's clearly passed his prime but still behaves
as if he's on top of the world.

00:01:15.650 --> 00:01:20.970
As it turns out, Leguizamo based his performance
on a real actor: former co-star, Steven Seagal.

00:01:20.970 --> 00:01:23.810
"If I find out you're lying I'm gonna come
back and kill you in your own kitchen."

00:01:24.217 --> 00:01:26.150
Leguizamo told The Ultimate Rabbit:

00:01:32.329 --> 00:01:36.479
As the story goes, Seagal shoved Leguizamo
against a wall after losing his temper.

00:01:36.479 --> 00:01:40.119
Leguizamo brought up the fact that he used
Seagal as his inspiration for The Menu in

00:01:40.119 --> 00:01:43.979
another interview with Entertainment Weekly,
confirming that the film they clashed on was

00:01:43.979 --> 00:01:48.210
1996's Executive Decision, one of Seagal's
better-rated movies.

00:01:48.210 --> 00:01:49.530
Leguizamo told the outlet:

00:01:53.497 --> 00:01:56.880
One of the central tensions in The Menu is
the fact that Margot doesn't want to eat the

00:01:56.880 --> 00:02:00.240
food Chef Slowik has so painstakingly prepared
for her.

00:02:00.240 --> 00:02:03.880
Tyler, on the other hand, who's played by
Nicholas Hoult, is in his element.

00:02:03.880 --> 00:02:07.591
What this meant for Anya Taylor-Joy was that
she didn't have to spend much of the film

00:02:07.591 --> 00:02:09.709
eating, unlike Hoult.

00:02:09.709 --> 00:02:12.959
Taylor-Joy discussed how impressed she was
with Hoult's eating abilities during an interview

00:02:12.959 --> 00:02:18.160
with BBC Radio 1, revealing that he "attacked
every take with so much gusto and fervor"

00:02:18.160 --> 00:02:19.980
and somehow managed to keep it all down.

00:02:19.980 --> 00:02:24.120
Hoult told Vanity Fair that the hardest day
on set was when he gorged himself on bread,

00:02:24.120 --> 00:02:27.780
a day that Taylor-Joy described as "BreadGate."

00:02:27.780 --> 00:02:31.989
If the high-dining experience shown in The
Menu feels very real, that's because a real-life

00:02:31.989 --> 00:02:34.790
superstar chef was a consultant on the film.

00:02:34.790 --> 00:02:39.349
French chef Dominique Crenn - the only woman
in the U.S. with a three-Michelin-star restaurant

00:02:39.349 --> 00:02:42.780
- worked to make the menu described in the
script a reality.

00:02:42.780 --> 00:02:46.459
Crenn crafted many of the dishes herself,
often re-doing them several times when scenes

00:02:46.459 --> 00:02:49.569
required numerous takes, according to The
Hollywood Reporter.

00:02:49.569 --> 00:02:53.610
One dish, which features a scallop balanced
atop a rock, is a near-replica of a dish at

00:02:53.610 --> 00:02:56.775
Crenn's own restaurant, San Francisco's Atelier
Crenn.

00:02:56.775 --> 00:02:58.161
Crenn told The Hollywood Reporter:

00:03:02.304 --> 00:03:06.470
She also spoke with Ralph Fiennes about the
psychological aspects of his character, describing

00:03:06.470 --> 00:03:10.330
how "mentally exhausting" it is to be the
head of a restaurant of that stature.

00:03:10.330 --> 00:03:13.370
Does Crenn see herself as a mad genius like
Chef Slowik?

00:03:13.370 --> 00:03:14.500
The chef told Bon Appétit:

00:03:18.528 --> 00:03:21.409
Nicholas Hoult's Tyler is the resident foodie
in The Menu.

00:03:21.409 --> 00:03:24.739
He's not a chef himself, but he's obsessed
with the world of food.

00:03:24.739 --> 00:03:28.720
As such, Tyler has obviously watched every
episode of the Netflix docu-series Chef's

00:03:28.720 --> 00:03:31.159
Table, something he proudly admits to.

00:03:31.159 --> 00:03:35.150
If you've watched Chef's Table yourself, you
might have noticed some similarities between

00:03:35.150 --> 00:03:37.780
how that series and The Menu were shot.

00:03:37.780 --> 00:03:42.019
These similarities were intentional: Chef's
Table creator David Gelb was actually hired

00:03:42.019 --> 00:03:45.980
as a second-unit director on the film, reports
The New York Times.

00:03:45.980 --> 00:03:50.040
Gelb's entire purpose for being on set was
to film the restaurant and the staff in exactly

00:03:50.040 --> 00:03:52.519
the same way an episode of Chef's Table would
be shot.

00:03:52.519 --> 00:03:56.900
Fans of the series will likely recognize the
slow-motion close-ups and the focus on small

00:03:56.900 --> 00:04:00.890
details - the bright heat of a blue flame,
the precision of a hand laying down a tiny

00:04:00.890 --> 00:04:02.480
piece of food on a plate.

00:04:02.480 --> 00:04:05.239
The film's climax is also a reference to Chef's
Table.

00:04:05.239 --> 00:04:09.470
Director Mike Mylod told The Hollywood Reporter
that the final s'mores dish was an homage

00:04:09.470 --> 00:04:14.590
to a dessert made by Grant Achatz at his restaurant
in Chicago, which was featured in an episode

00:04:14.590 --> 00:04:15.590
of Chef's Table.

00:04:15.590 --> 00:04:19.900
Achatz's dessert is laid out over the entire
table, which Mylod expanded to encompass the

00:04:19.900 --> 00:04:21.250
entire restaurant.

00:04:21.250 --> 00:04:25.321
The aerial shot of the s'mores dish is a tribute
to that specific dessert, but also to the

00:04:25.321 --> 00:04:28.440
cinematography of Chef's Table as a whole.

00:04:28.440 --> 00:04:32.460
The Menu is quite a mysterious film in which
the backgrounds of some characters are left

00:04:32.460 --> 00:04:33.490
in the dark.

00:04:33.490 --> 00:04:37.810
That's especially true of Elsa, the austere
hostess character played by Hong Chau.

00:04:37.810 --> 00:04:41.240
Speaking with Awards Radar, Chau described
the process of deepening the character of

00:04:41.240 --> 00:04:45.130
Elsa, which involved a lot of conversations
with director Mike Mylod.

00:04:45.130 --> 00:04:48.600
She explained that she was shooting a movie
in Portland, Oregon when she got the role,

00:04:48.600 --> 00:04:51.650
and she wanted to bring some of the city's
"funkiness" to the character.

00:04:51.650 --> 00:04:56.020
Chau had her own ideas about how Elsa should
look, though Mylod wasn't especially receptive

00:04:56.020 --> 00:04:57.590
to them, at least at first.

00:04:57.590 --> 00:04:58.870
Chau told Awards Radar:

00:05:05.900 --> 00:05:10.590
Luckily, Chau found an ally in Amy Westcott,
the film's costume designer — and also the

00:05:10.590 --> 00:05:11.750
director's wife.

00:05:11.750 --> 00:05:15.830
Amy Westcott "was very willing to conspire
with me against Mike Mylod," Chau went on

00:05:15.830 --> 00:05:17.240
to explain to the outlet.

00:05:17.240 --> 00:05:20.990
The outfit they ended up with — a sort of
modern Victorian look - is unique enough to

00:05:20.990 --> 00:05:25.020
give Elsa some personality, but not too zany
that it contradicts the serious atmosphere

00:05:25.020 --> 00:05:26.810
at the restaurant.

00:05:26.810 --> 00:05:31.590
The Menu was written by Seth Reiss and Will
Tracy, two comedians who have plenty of experience

00:05:31.590 --> 00:05:32.690
in satire.

00:05:32.690 --> 00:05:36.819
Reiss spent several years at Late Night With
Seth Meyers and Tracy worked on Succession,

00:05:36.819 --> 00:05:39.400
and both screenwriters previously worked for
The Onion.

00:05:39.400 --> 00:05:43.580
Of all their career experiences, it was their
time at the satirical news website that proved

00:05:43.580 --> 00:05:46.690
to be most influential in the making of The
Menu.

00:05:46.690 --> 00:05:48.120
Reiss told Slash Film:

00:05:56.660 --> 00:06:01.190
Judith Light is a veteran Hollywood actor,
known for her vast body of work on television,

00:06:01.190 --> 00:06:03.080
in film, and on Broadway.

00:06:03.080 --> 00:06:07.080
She has a small but important part in The
Menu, that of Anne, a wealthy woman who frequents

00:06:07.080 --> 00:06:11.020
the restaurant with her husband but doesn't
seem to enjoy the food all that much.

00:06:11.020 --> 00:06:14.490
Speaking with Cinema Daily, Light explained
that she's a foodie herself, something that

00:06:14.490 --> 00:06:16.030
drew her to the role.

00:06:16.030 --> 00:06:17.030
Light told the outlet:

00:06:27.080 --> 00:06:30.650
Light went on to say that she grew up in "a
family of female cooks," which is why it was

00:06:30.650 --> 00:06:35.180
such a pleasure to see the famous French chef
Dominique Crenn in action during the shoot.

00:06:35.180 --> 00:06:36.380
The actor told Cinema Daily:

00:06:46.509 --> 00:06:50.690
Certain roles require actors to do intense
prep work, whether it be learning a new skill,

00:06:50.690 --> 00:06:53.250
perfecting an accent, or transforming their
bodies.

00:06:53.250 --> 00:06:57.120
For the stars of The Menu, preparation for
the film was far more enjoyable.

00:06:57.120 --> 00:06:59.539
Speaking with The Hollywood Reporter, Anya
Taylor-Joy joked:

00:06:59.539 --> 00:07:01.790
"This was a very hard job in terms of research.

00:07:01.790 --> 00:07:05.319
Both Nick and I spent a lot of time on the
couch watching 'Chef's Table.'

00:07:05.319 --> 00:07:06.900
It was really hard."

00:07:06.900 --> 00:07:10.680
Nicholas Hoult's Tyler is obsessed with shows
like Chef's Table, so binge-watching the series

00:07:10.680 --> 00:07:12.500
was always going to be helpful for him.

00:07:12.500 --> 00:07:14.270
However, he didn't stop there.

00:07:14.270 --> 00:07:17.879
Taylor-Joy also revealed that Hoult went even
further in his research, actually going out

00:07:17.879 --> 00:07:20.690
to eat at some of these fine-dining restaurants
himself.

00:07:20.690 --> 00:07:21.690
Hoult joked:

00:07:21.690 --> 00:07:23.729
"I went and ate nice food, because I had to."

00:07:23.729 --> 00:07:27.730
Anya Taylor-Joy and Nicholas Hoult have shared
that they did a lot of improvising while their

00:07:27.730 --> 00:07:31.449
characters were sitting in the background,
but there was one moment of improvisation

00:07:31.449 --> 00:07:33.020
that was front and center.

00:07:33.020 --> 00:07:37.140
Near the end of the film, Chef Slowik reveals
to Margot the extent of Tyler's deception:

00:07:37.140 --> 00:07:41.600
He had known all along how the night would
end and invited Margot anyway.

00:07:41.600 --> 00:07:44.810
When Margot hears this, she slaps Tyler across
the face.

00:07:44.810 --> 00:07:49.280
As Nicholas Hoult told BBC Radio 1, Tyler
definitely deserved a slap in the face, but

00:07:49.280 --> 00:07:51.330
that moment wasn't actually in the script.

00:07:51.330 --> 00:07:53.500
Anya Taylor Joy explained her decision, saying,

00:07:53.500 --> 00:07:56.229
"I have a thing about feminine rage."

00:07:56.229 --> 00:07:59.520
She went on to reveal that she often gets
scripts that have men doing terrible things

00:07:59.520 --> 00:08:03.690
to women, and the women just sitting there
and absorbing those terrible things, upset

00:08:03.690 --> 00:08:05.060
but not angry.

00:08:05.060 --> 00:08:08.420
After reading the big reveal moment in the
script, Taylor-Joy went up to director Mark

00:08:08.420 --> 00:08:10.040
Mylod and told him,

00:08:10.040 --> 00:08:15.440
"I'm really sorry, but the only way to play
this truthfully is for me to like, attack

00:08:15.440 --> 00:08:16.440
him."

00:08:16.440 --> 00:08:19.960
Mylod eventually came around to Taylor-Joy's
way of thinking, and she got to react to Tyler's

00:08:19.960 --> 00:08:21.630
betrayal authentically.

00:08:21.630 --> 00:08:23.220
How did Hoult feel about the slap?

00:08:23.220 --> 00:08:24.220
The actor joked:

00:08:24.220 --> 00:08:25.220
"I didn't like it.

00:08:25.220 --> 00:08:27.000
I didn't like it one bit."

00:08:27.000 --> 00:08:30.879
Chef Slowik's menu for the evening was meticulously
planned out, which is part of the reason why

00:08:30.879 --> 00:08:34.399
he doesn't take too kindly to the unexpected
appearance of Margot.

00:08:34.399 --> 00:08:38.849
To make matters worse, Margot is entirely
unreceptive to his brand of culinary genius

00:08:38.849 --> 00:08:42.959
and refuses to play along as one of his dutiful,
awestruck guests.

00:08:42.959 --> 00:08:46.600
Most of Margot's stubbornness is in the script,
of course, but Anya Taylor-Joy made a character

00:08:46.600 --> 00:08:50.190
choice on set that made her dislike of him
even more explicit.

00:08:50.190 --> 00:08:53.660
Unfortunately, she put herself in a bit of
pain in doing so.

00:08:53.660 --> 00:08:57.351
She told BBC Radio 1 that she thought it was
important that Margot have her back to the

00:08:57.351 --> 00:09:00.380
chef in order to illustrate how she really
feels about him.

00:09:00.380 --> 00:09:04.430
What this meant in practice was that Taylor-Joy
had to be constantly twisting around to look

00:09:04.430 --> 00:09:07.649
at Chef Slowik while he delivered his monologues
about the food.

00:09:07.649 --> 00:09:08.649
She told the outlet:

00:09:08.649 --> 00:09:13.410
"That essentially meant three months of the
worst neck pain you could possibly imagine."

00:09:13.410 --> 00:09:17.800
Taylor-Joy expanded on the experience further
in a video segment for Vanity Fair, noting

00:09:17.800 --> 00:09:19.620
that, despite the back problems it gave her:

00:09:19.620 --> 00:09:21.516
"I still maintain that it was a good choice."

